======================================================================
STEP 5: combine -- exact closure, UPPER bound (explicit IVT)
======================================================================
a(n):=max_x h5(n,x) is continuous in real n>4 (Berge's maximum
theorem: max of a jointly continuous function over the compact
set x in [0,1]).
a(7) = max_x h5(7,x) = 0.3333333333333333333333333  at x* = 1.00000000000000
a(7) < M5  CONFIRMED (exact).

EXPLICIT IVT ARGUMENT (stated completely, from the start):
For real n > n0_upper := max(n0_boundary, max_root_upper), a(n)
never equals M5 (boundary: STEP 3, valid for n>n0_boundary=6.30;
interior: STEP 4, valid for n>max_root_upper). a(n) is continuous
on this connected interval, and a(7) < M5 at one point of it.
By the Intermediate Value Theorem, a CONTINUOUS function that is
NEVER EQUAL to M5 on a connected interval, and is < M5 at one
point of that interval, is < M5 at EVERY point of that interval
(if it were >= M5 anywhere else, continuity + never-equal would
force it to cross M5 somewhere in between, a contradiction).
Hence a(n) < M5 for ALL real n > n0_upper, in particular for
every integer n in the claimed domain.

>>> UPPER-BOUND THEOREM (K=5, EXACT): for all integer n>=7 and
>>> x in [0,1]:  n*Delta_n(x) <= M5,  M5 = 0.696803198946355211196876665384

